= Metabolism =

== Overview ==
'''Metabolism''' is the network of chemical reactions that occur in living cells and support maintenance, growth, reproduction and responses to the environment. It includes reactions that release usable energy, reactions that require energy input, and the regulation and exchange of metabolites among pathways.

== Catabolism and anabolism ==
'''Catabolic''' pathways break down or transform molecules and can be coupled to the conservation of usable energy. '''Anabolic''' pathways synthesize cellular components from smaller precursors and generally require energy or reducing power. These classes are not isolated: cells connect them by shared metabolites, cofactors and regulatory mechanisms.

== Energy coupling ==
ATP, reducing cofactors and electrochemical gradients link some energy-releasing reactions to energy-requiring processes. The energetics of a single reaction depend on chemical conditions, not on whether a pathway is informally described as “building” or “breaking down.” [[Related to::Thermodynamics|Thermodynamic constraints]] govern possible energy transformations, and [[Related to::Enzyme|enzymes]] accelerate reactions without changing their equilibrium constants at a fixed temperature.

== Diversity of metabolism ==
Metabolic pathways vary by organism, tissue and environment. Oxygen-dependent respiration is important in many organisms but is not universal; anaerobic respiration and fermentation provide other ways to support energy transfer. Photosynthetic organisms also conduct metabolism, and [[Related to::Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] can supply chemical energy and carbon compounds that feed other pathways.

== Regulation and homeostasis ==
[[Related to::Homeostasis|Homeostasis]] requires sustained exchanges and regulation. Metabolic fluxes respond to substrate availability, energy demand, feedback mechanisms, cellular compartmentalization, enzymes and environmental conditions. A steady metabolite concentration need not imply that its synthesis and consumption have stopped.

== Scientific inference ==
A metabolic map alone is not proof that every pathway carries measurable flux in a given cell. Evidence may require isotope tracing, metabolite measurements, perturbation experiments, enzyme activities or flux-model constraints. Observations must specify system, time, cell type, nutrient conditions and uncertainty.

